          "settings": {
            "heading": "Choose bead weight, size, and shape",
            "heading_size": "h2"
          }
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Beads add weight and a clean head. Material decides how fast the fly gets down. Shape and hole style decide how it seats on the hook.</p><h3>Materials and sink rate</h3><ul><li><strong>Tungsten:</strong> denser. Same size sinks faster than brass, so you can stay compact in deeper or faster water.</li><li><strong>Brass:</strong> moderate sink. Useful when you want depth control without a heavy head.</li><li><strong>Glass:</strong> light, a bit of translucence. Good when you want color more than weight.</li></ul><h3>Tungsten vs brass</h3><p>Tungsten is denser, so a smaller bead gets down faster. Brass is lighter and gives a slower fall in shallow or soft water. Football and slotted shapes change how the bead seats on jig hooks. Buy the density for the seam, not the shinier bag.</p><h3>Hole style</h3><p>Slotted beads open to the side so they seat on jig hooks and straight shanks. Through-hole beads slide over a conventional eye. Seat the bead flush behind the eye without crowding the thorax.</p><h3>Sizing we stock</h3><ul><li>1.5-2.0 mm: small midges and light nymphs</li><li>2.4-2.8 mm: standard nymphs</li><li>3.3-3.8 mm: bigger nymphs and small streamers</li><li>5.5 mm: Spawn football beads</li><li>8-9 mm: Spawn Fathom beads</li></ul><h3>Shapes beyond the round head</h3><p>Cyclops and painted beads for classic nymphs. Lead and dumbbell eyes when you want a streamer to ride correctly. Football and Fathom profiles when you want a different silhouette than a plain ball. Match the head to the pattern, not the other way around.</p><p>Pair beads with <a href=\"/collections/fly-tying-materials\">Fly Tying Materials</a> and <a href=\"/collections/fly-tying-tools\">Fly Tying Tools</a>.</p>"
          }
        },
