    "faq": {
      "type": "collapsible-content",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Slotted vs non-slotted beads?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Slotted beads open to the side for jig hooks and straight shanks. Through-hole beads slide over a conventional eye. If the bead will not seat without forcing the hook, you have the wrong hole style. Full guide: <a href=\"/blogs/guides/slotted-vs-through-hole-beads\">Slotted vs Through-Hole Beads</a>.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Tungsten vs brass or glass: sink rate?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Tungsten sinks faster at a smaller diameter. Brass is moderate. Glass is light. In fast or deep water, tungsten usually gets you to the zone with less bulk.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How do I match bead size to hook?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Use the sizing list on this page. The bead should sit flush behind the eye without swallowing the thorax. If the head looks oversized for the hook, drop a size.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "When do I use eyes or cones instead of a round bead?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Dumbbell and lead eyes help streamers track and show a face. Cones and specialty heads change profile and weight distribution. Round beads are the default for most nymphs.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Will a heavier bead ruin the drift?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Too heavy and the fly digs or hangs. Too light and it never reaches fish. Start with the size chart for the pattern, then step tungsten up or down one size based on depth and current.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What is the difference between tungsten and brass beads?",
            "icon": "none",
            "row_content": "<p>Tungsten sinks faster at a smaller size. Brass sinks slower and suits shallower water. Same outer size does not mean the same weight.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "collapsible_row",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Where is the tungsten vs brass guide?",
            "icon": "none",
